  "Number": 17,
  "Question": "If \\(\\log(xy^3) = 1\\) and \\(\\log(x^2y) = 1,\\) what is \\(\\log(xy)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "B": "\\(0\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\)",
    "E": "\\(1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The product and power rules for logarithms turn the given equations into a simple linear system. First check that these rules apply: \\(x^2y>0\\) forces \\(x\\ne0\\) and \\(y>0\\), and then \\(xy^3>0\\) forces \\(x>0\\).\n\nLet \\(u=\\log x\\) and \\(v=\\log y\\). The equations become\n\\[\nu+3v=1,\\qquad 2u+v=1.\n\\]\nTwice the first equation minus the second eliminates \\(u\\):\n\\[\n5v=1.\n\\]\nThus \\(v=\\frac15\\), and substituting into \\(2u+v=1\\) gives \\(u=\\frac25\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y\n=\\frac25+\\frac15=\\boxed{\\frac35},\n\\]\nwhich is choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
